  "name": "Tyrolean Schuhplattler (slap-dance with music)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Tyrolean Schuhplattler folk-dance-and-music tradition of the Austrian Tyrol, South Tyrol (Italy), and Bavarian Alps, in which male dancers perform synchronized choreography involving rhythmic slapping of thighs, knees, and shoe-soles in characteristic accelerating patterns, accompanied by Stubenmusik chamber-ensemble of zither, hammered dulcimer (Hackbrett), accordion, double-bass, and voice. The tradition crystallized in seventeenth-eighteenth century Alpine villages from earlier courtship-display dance-forms. Performance contexts span the Heimatabend folkloric-evening tourist-demonstrations, village-festival celebrations, and the canonical Schuhplattlerverein dance-association competitions. Distinguished from broader German Alpine music by Schuhplattler's specific male-percussive-choreographic register.",
